# Institute migrations — one file, one apply command

## Objective

A product migration under `supabase/migrations/` is written once for schema `public`. The apply command decides what each institute receives. Do not keep a second rewritten SQL file.

## Command

```bash
npm run sql:apply-migration:all-tenants:pg -- supabase/migrations/<file>.sql
```

`npm run sql:apply-migration:all-tenants` calls that same script. Do not run `psql` once per institute. Do not paste the file into the Supabase SQL editor for each institute.

## IPv4 session pooler

`db.<ref>.supabase.co` often has no A record. This machine has no IPv6 route, so a direct connect fails with `ENOTFOUND`.

The apply command handles that. When the direct host has no IPv4 address, it opens `postgres.<ref>` on the session pooler (`*.pooler.supabase.com`, port 5432, `sslmode=no-verify`). It tries the cached host in `scripts/.institute-pooler-hosts.json` first, then the host list in `src/lib/tenant/institutePoolerUrl.ts`. A URL that already resolves over IPv4 stays as stored. Do not rewrite `platform_tenants.database_url` for this. Do not pass `--skip-dns-check` to hide the failure.

## What the command does

Targets come from Pulsar `platform_tenants` when `PULSAR_DATABASE_URL` is set. Otherwise they come from `TENANTS_CONFIG_JSON`.

| `data_mode` | What runs |
|---|---|
| `project` (default) | The git file, unchanged, on that institute `database_url`. Success is stored in `institute_migration_ledger.applied` with `schema_name` `public`. |
| `shared_schema` | An in-memory rewrite: `public.` and `search_path = public` become `postgres_schema` (`tenant_<slug>`). `auth.` and `storage.` stay. Success is stored in `institute_migration_ledger.applied` on that database with `schema_name` `postgres_schema`. |

Brand-data files still run on `project` targets. They are skipped on `shared_schema`. A brand-data file starts with `-- ge:brand-data <slug>`. Enum and kind migrations are product SQL. They are not brand data.

A shared-schema target rejects the file when a `table`, `function`, `view`, `type`, or `sequence` is created without a `public.` qualifier. Policies, indexes, and triggers may stay unqualified when their `ON` target is qualified.

## When you add a migration

1. Write the SQL for `public`. Qualify product tables, functions, views, types, and sequences with `public.`.
2. Keep the file additive (`21-migrations-production-no-data-destruction.mdc`).
3. Run the command above for that file.
4. Do not edit the file into `tenant_*` form.
5. Do not put this logic in `supabase/pulsar-migrations/`. Those files are the control plane only.

`data_mode` and `postgres_schema` live on `platform_tenants` (`supabase/pulsar-migrations/006_institute_data_plane.sql`). Missing columns mean every row is `project` / `public`.

## Checklist

- [ ] New product objects use `public.`?
- [ ] Apply went through `sql:apply-migration:all-tenants:pg`? A direct host with no IPv4 address used the session pooler. The log line is `IPv4 pooler <host>`.
- [ ] Shared-schema failure names the institute and the code (`unqualified_public`, `invalid_tenant_schema`)?
